The row can be updated from right to left, so each new value still uses the old value from the previous position. Starting with a row full of `1`s also takes care of both edges.

For `n = 1`, only `[1]` is returned. The code stores a copy of each finished row because the working row changes again on the next loop.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> pascalTriangleIII(int n) {
        List<List<Integer>> triangle = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> currentRow = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int rowIndex = 0; rowIndex < n; rowIndex++) {
            currentRow.add(1);

            for (int column = rowIndex - 1; column > 0; column--) {
                int updatedValue = currentRow.get(column)
                                  + currentRow.get(column - 1);
                currentRow.set(column, updatedValue);
            }

            triangle.add(new ArrayList<>(currentRow));
        }

        return triangle;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the returned rows, with `O(n)` extra working space.

Each row starts and ends with `1`. The values in the middle come from the two numbers in the row above, so the triangle can be filled from top to bottom.

For `n = 4`, the rows are `[1]`, `[1, 1]`, `[1, 2, 1]`, and `[1, 3, 3, 1]`. The code also returns an empty result when no rows are requested.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> pascalTriangleIII(int n) {
        vector<vector<int>> triangle;

        for (int rowIndex = 0; rowIndex < n; rowIndex++) {
            vector<int> row(rowIndex + 1, 1);

            for (int column = 1; column < rowIndex; column++) {
                row[column] = triangle[rowIndex - 1][column - 1]
                            + triangle[rowIndex - 1][column];
            }

            triangle.push_back(row);
        }

        return triangle;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because all values in the triangle are created.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the returned triangle.

The recursive call builds all rows before the current one. After getting the smaller triangle, the last row is used to create one more row by adding neighboring values.

The empty input case is handled before recursion. This version is easy to follow, but it also uses the call stack, so an iterative version is safer for a very large `n`.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> pascalTriangleIII(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        if (n == 1) {
            List<List<Integer>> firstTriangle = new ArrayList<>();
            firstTriangle.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1)));
            return firstTriangle;
        }

        List<List<Integer>> triangle = pascalTriangleIII(n - 1);
        List<Integer> previousRow = triangle.get(triangle.size() - 1);
        List<Integer> currentRow = new ArrayList<>();

        currentRow.add(1);
        for (int i = 1; i < previousRow.size(); i++) {
            currentRow.add(previousRow.get(i - 1) + previousRow.get(i));
        }
        currentRow.add(1);

        triangle.add(currentRow);
        return triangle;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the output, plus `O(n)` recursive stack space.

